generateTriangulations -- generate all triangulations with certain properties
*****************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        Ts = generateTriangulations A
        Ts = generateTriangulations(A, tri)
        Ts = generateTriangulations T
  * Inputs:
      * A, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, over the integers (or
        rationals?), whose columns are the points to use
      * tri, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, A list of lists of indices
        representing a triangulation of the columns of $A$
      * T, an instance of the type Triangulation (missing documentation),
        packages both $A$ and tri in one object
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Limit => an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, default value infinity,
        Stop after constructing this many triangulations
      * RegularOnly => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default
        value false, Only generate regular triangulations
      * Homogenize => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default
        value true, set to false in the case the columns form a vector
        configuration. The default case is that the columns of $A$ are
        considered a point configuration (not used in the variant
        generateTriangulations T)
  * Outputs:
      * Ts, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, of lists of integers, each such
        list represents a triangulation

Description
===========

This function can be used to generate a set of triangulations of a point set or
vector configuration $A$ (the points are the columns of $A$).

It operates by starting with one triangulation (tri or T), if one is given, and
if not, it constructs a fine triangulation of the set of columns of $A$.

After this, it uses bistellar flips to generate neighbors, and continues, until
the limit is reached, or no new ones can be constructed.

Important note! This function generally starts with a fine triangulation (i.e.
one using all of the points in $A$), and only considers bistellar flips that
give fine triangulations.

